// What init/1 holds up: the waits on its own stack while its
// supervisor's start waits on it.
//
// init/1 of a process module runs inside its starter's start: a
// supervisor's, or whoever called start_child. A distributed operation
// init/1 runs on its own stack holds that start, and is startup's finding
// (a stalled start); the same site anywhere else is blocking's (a stalled
// server). Both analyses read the walks here, so a site is init's finding
// or blocking's, never both and never neither. global_reach.dl is the
// :global half (global_call_during_init); this is the rpc half
// (rpc_during_init).
//
// The phase ends where init/1 acknowledges its start: a
// `:proc_lib.init_ack/1,2`, after which the starter is released and what
// init/1 goes on to run is the server's (acked_edge), or a
// `:gen_server.enter_loop` that makes it the server. Every walk here, and
// init_dep in entries.dl, stops there.
//
// Included by otp.dl, after calls.dl and entries.dl.

// ── Where the phase ends ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `func`'s edge to `callee` is taken only after `func` acknowledged its
// start: every call it makes to callee runs after its own init_ack
// (start_acked) — logger_olp's init/1 acks, then enters its loop — or
// the callee is a gen_server or gen_statem enter_loop, which does not
// return: what that loop waits for is the server's, wherever it is
// entered from. An enter_loop reached before any ack is startup's
// "enter_loop" finding. An ack made in a helper init/1 calls is not seen
// (start_acked is per function).
.decl acked_edge(func: symbol, callee: symbol)

acked_edge(f, g) :- call_instr(f, g, id), start_acked(id, f), !unacked_call(f, g).
acked_edge(f, g) :- call_edge(f, g), match(":gen_(server|statem):enter_loop/[0-9]+", g).

.decl unacked_call(func: symbol, callee: symbol)

unacked_call(f, g) :- call_instr(f, g, id), !start_acked(id, f).

// init/1 functions whose own stack reaches an acknowledgement: their
// phase ends before everything they run returns. Few: most inits are a
// gen_server's, acknowledged when they return.
.init ack_reach = SameProcessReachSet

ack_reach.seed(f) :- acked_edge(f, _).

.decl acks_in_init(init: symbol)

acks_in_init(i) :- init_function(_, i), ack_reach.reaches(i).

// What such an init/1 runs before its start returns: its own stack,
// less the edges after the ack and the side paths (side_call, calls.dl).
// Forward from those few inits; the others' phase is all they reach.
.init before_ack = ForwardSameProcessReachCut

before_ack.root(i, i) :- acks_in_init(i).
before_ack.cut(f, g) :- acked_edge(f, g).
before_ack.cut(f, g) :- side_call(f, g).

// `init` is init/1 of a process module, and the rpc at `site` runs on its
// stack: in init/1, or in anything init/1 calls in its own process (not
// what a spawn, task or agent runs: runs_elsewhere), not through the
// logging or telemetry API (side_call, calls.dl), and before init/1
// acknowledges its start (acked_edge; an rpc its own function makes
// after its ack is not one).
.decl rpc_during_init(init: symbol, site: symbol)

.init init_rpc_reach = SameProcessReachCut

init_rpc_reach.cut(f, g) :- side_call(f, g).
init_rpc_reach.cut(f, g) :- acked_edge(f, g).
init_rpc_reach.seed(g, site) :- rpc_call(site, g, _, _), !start_acked(site, g).

rpc_during_init(init, site) :- init_function(_, init), init_rpc_reach.reaches(init, site).
